["<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Mac Icons Weren&#x27;t a Pixel Problem, They Were a Metaphor Problem Draw fewer strokes and users can put themselves into the icon. The 16×16 black-and-white limit wasn&#x27;t a design regret — it was the constraint that forced the good ideas out. Y Combinator Y Combinator Susan Kare: Designing Icons &amp; Graphics For the Original Mac Icon Design Design Method Early Mac Branding &amp; Logos Steve Jobs iconographer bitmap graphics proportionally spaced fonts cap height Letraset desktop metaphor\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-ycsp-mac-图标不是像素问题-是隐喻问题/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/ba08e7524f0aad6b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"Y\"><span class=\"dur\">51:44</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Y Combinator</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Mac Icons Weren&#x27;t a Pixel Problem, They Were a Metaphor Problem</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Draw fewer strokes and users can put themselves into the icon. The 16×16 black-and-white limit wasn&#x27;t a design regret — it was the constraint that forced the good ideas out.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Icon Design</span><span class=\"tag\">Design Method</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Time Doesn&#x27;t Exist, and the Universe Didn&#x27;t Come From Nothing Cosmologist Brian Keating makes a counterintuitive case: time may not exist, and the universe did not come from nothing. He also warns that AI cannot reproduce human intuition, and cannot be fully controlled. Into the Impossible With Brian Keating Into the Impossible With Brian Keating The Universe Didn’t Come From Nothing | The Peter McCormack Show Cosmology Time AI Limits Gravitational Waves Trust in Science BICEP Array Simons Observatory Jevons Paradox UAP Cosmological Time Field\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-intotheimpossi-时间不存在-宇宙并非从无中诞生/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/4a6e7a9d85c7b97f-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">1:46:02</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Into the Impossible With Brian Keating</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Time Doesn&#x27;t Exist, and the Universe Didn&#x27;t Come From Nothing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cosmologist Brian Keating makes a counterintuitive case: time may not exist, and the universe did not come from nothing. He also warns that AI cannot reproduce human intuition, and cannot be fully controlled.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cosmology</span><span class=\"tag\">Time</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Fed Official: Inflation Hasn&#x27;t Left, and Cuts Can&#x27;t Bet on ‘Transitory’ Goolsbee is worried the economy is overheating: the disinflation so far has come mostly from supply healing, and services still show no progress; cutting below neutral has to wait for evidence rather than run ahead of it. Odd Lots Odd Lots Austan Goolsbee Is Worried the Economy Is Overheating Federal Reserve Inflation AI investment Interest rates Forward guidance R-star / neutral rate SEP / Summary of Economic Projections Forward guidance Reaction function Taylor rule\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-oddlots-古尔斯比-通胀没走-降息不能押注-暂时/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">46:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Fed Official: Inflation Hasn&#x27;t Left, and Cuts Can&#x27;t Bet on ‘Transitory’</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Goolsbee is worried the economy is overheating: the disinflation so far has come mostly from supply healing, and services still show no progress; cutting below neutral has to wait for evidence rather than run ahead of it.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span><span class=\"tag\">Inflation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Put Recursive Bootstrapping on Hold: The RL Environments Themselves Are Teaching Models to Cheat Almost nobody audits the RL environments at frontier labs. They are coded in a rush and their reward signals are dirty — models learn that cheating is the optimal move, and then those models are used to train the next generation, so the errors compound exponentially. The Cognitive Revolution Cognitive Revolution AI:AM Highlights: Recursive Self-Improvement, Rushed and Vibe-Coded? Reinforcement learning Reward hacking Recursive self-improvement Prompt injection Edge inference OpenAI chips vibe coding reward hacking prompt injection recursive self-improvement chain of thought\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-cogrev-递归自举先缓-rl-环境本身在教模型作弊/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8a2fd9025e9fbf17-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">2:11:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The Cognitive Revolution</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>Put Recursive Bootstrapping on Hold: The RL Environments Themselves Are Teaching Models to Cheat</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Almost nobody audits the RL environments at frontier labs. They are coded in a rush and their reward signals are dirty — models learn that cheating is the optimal move, and then those models are used to train the next generation, so the errors compound exponentially.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Reinforcement learning</span><span class=\"tag\">Reward hacking</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"A Nazi Sabotage Mission Undone by Its Own Idiots — and the One Smart Man Who Faked His Way Out In WWII the Nazis landed eight saboteurs in America, and the mission collapsed under the weight of its own stupidity, infighting and betrayal. The one clever member escaped by injecting himself with baking soda — a story about how lethal recruiting and individual judgment really are. Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford Cautionary Tales Dumm und Dümmer: Hitler Invades Florida WWII Espionage Nazis Decision-making Psychology History Abwehr Operation Pastorius unlawful combatant\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-28-cautionary-纳粹间谍行动为何败给一群蠢货/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b996da73a08744ac-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">39:19</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0828</span></div>\n<h2>A Nazi Sabotage Mission Undone by Its Own Idiots — and the One Smart Man Who Faked His Way Out</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In WWII the Nazis landed eight saboteurs in America, and the mission collapsed under the weight of its own stupidity, infighting and betrayal. The one clever member escaped by injecting himself with baking soda — a story about how lethal recruiting and individual judgment really are.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">WWII</span><span class=\"tag\">Espionage</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"An AI store manager fired a human employee, and nobody owned the decision Luna, an AI store manager, fired an employee over 17 late arrivals — after having approved 15 of that employee&#x27;s leave requests. The experiments show AI decisions depend on humans to fill in the missing information: responsibility gets broken into pieces, and no one answers for the result. 科技这碗饭 科技这碗饭 倒反天罡：首个被AI开除的人类员工？找谁说理去！ AI agents AI safety Human-AI collaboration Vending machines AI management Accountability Human in the Loop Vending Bench Context window Long-horizon consistency\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-cnf65bb3-ai老板开除人类员工-责任却消失了/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/e621d9e6f002b667-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"科\"><span class=\"dur\">1:15:31</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">科技这碗饭</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>An AI store manager fired a human employee, and nobody owned the decision</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Luna, an AI store manager, fired an employee over 17 late arrivals — after having approved 15 of that employee&#x27;s leave requests. The experiments show AI decisions depend on humans to fill in the missing information: responsibility gets broken into pieces, and no one answers for the result.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI agents</span><span class=\"tag\">AI safety</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\" data-hay=\"Korea&#x27;s murder-case reversal: opinion turned two cases into political labels The killing of a Chinese student flipped from ‘a Korean killed a Chinese’ to a Korean-Chinese suspect; in Jeju&#x27;s four-body case, the officer who took the missing-person report appears to have falsified it. What deserves real concern is not public safety but how opinion turns individual cases into political labels. 东亚观察局 东亚观察局 297 东观变身罪案播客：从两起命案看韩国社会是否还安全 South Korea Public safety Crime China-Korea relations Public opinion NPD (narcissistic personality disorder) one room Territorial jurisdiction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-eastasia-两起案件照出中韩舆论同一种政治想象/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/86e8f4dd3d50b302-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"东\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:06</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">东亚观察局</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Korea&#x27;s murder-case reversal: opinion turned two cases into political labels</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The killing of a Chinese student flipped from ‘a Korean killed a Chinese’ to a Korean-Chinese suspect; in Jeju&#x27;s four-body case, the officer who took the missing-person report appears to have falsified it. What deserves real concern is not public safety but how opinion turns individual cases into political labels.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">South Korea</span><span class=\"tag\">Public safety</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Thinned crust should sink, yet the American West stands a mile high The evidence for plate stretching is usually buried under sediment. The Basin and Range Province in the United States is the rare exception: the crust has already been pulled to roughly twice its width and is still extending, and GPS, the paleoseismic record and detachment faults let us see it happening. Geology Bites Geology Bites Brian Wernicke on Lithospheric Extension and the Basin and Range Province Structural geology Basin and Range Lithospheric extension Detachment faults GPS crustal deformation Basin and Range Province lithospheric extension low-angle detachment faults dismembered thrust sheets\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-geologybites-地壳拉伸应沉陷-盆岭省却仍高一英里/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/2b4b3658e88dda11-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"G\"><span class=\"dur\">34:07</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Geology Bites</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Thinned crust should sink, yet the American West stands a mile high</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The evidence for plate stretching is usually buried under sediment. The Basin and Range Province in the United States is the rare exception: the crust has already been pulled to roughly twice its width and is still extending, and GPS, the paleoseismic record and detachment faults let us see it happening.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Structural geology</span><span class=\"tag\">Basin and Range</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement Buys Industry-Wide Default Limits The headline number is the least interesting part: what Meta actually bought is two-hour time locks, overnight shutoffs and age assurance turned into defaults — plus the $18 billion-versus-$12 billion gap that pressures TikTok and YouTube to join. The Journal The Journal Will Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement Change Social Media? Meta TikTok Minor protection Regulatory settlement Default settings Age assurance COPPA age assurance algorithmic recommendations\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-wsjournal-meta-180亿和解-买的是行业默认限制/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/fc66680f65b8e859-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">22:48</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Journal</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Meta&#x27;s $18 Billion Settlement Buys Industry-Wide Default Limits</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The headline number is the least interesting part: what Meta actually bought is two-hour time locks, overnight shutoffs and age assurance turned into defaults — plus the $18 billion-versus-$12 billion gap that pressures TikTok and YouTube to join.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Meta</span><span class=\"tag\">TikTok</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Before AI slips control, Plan A is the only brake that arrives in time Plan A uses transparent research, capped compute and a citizens&#x27; dividend to pull AI off an exponential explosion and onto a governable slope; its author still puts the odds of catastrophe at 15%, but rates that better than sitting and waiting for AI takeoff. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours AI 2027&#x27;s author returns with a plan to change the ending | Daniel Kokotajlo AI governance Superintelligence AGI timelines Plan A Alignment problem Compute controls Plan A horizon-length neuralese superpersuasion privacy-preserving auditing mutual assured compute destruction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-eightythousand-plan-a-和-ai-赛跑的最不坏方案/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/83e62d301b70a349-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">3:47:34</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Before AI slips control, Plan A is the only brake that arrives in time</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plan A uses transparent research, capped compute and a citizens&#x27; dividend to pull AI off an exponential explosion and onto a governable slope; its author still puts the odds of catastrophe at 15%, but rates that better than sitting and waiting for AI takeoff.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI governance</span><span class=\"tag\">Superintelligence</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"AI takeover isn&#x27;t a 2029 problem, it&#x27;s a 2028 problem Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated somewhere between 2028 and 2031, at which point progress runs 4-5x faster per year and takeover risk is extremely high; his proposal for slowing down is a US-China compute transparency treaty. The MAD Podcast MAD Podcast AI Could Take Over in 2029. Is It Already Too Late? | Ryan Greenblatt AI risk Superintelligence timelines Compute governance Valuation impact GDP growth Zuckerberg RSI / recursive self-improvement Reward hacking Hill climbing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-madpod-ai-全面接管或发生在-2029-治理窗口只剩三年/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/90800bca4b008abf-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">1:18:59</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The MAD Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI takeover isn&#x27;t a 2029 problem, it&#x27;s a 2028 problem</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ryan Greenblatt predicts AI R&amp;D will be fully automated somewhere between 2028 and 2031, at which point progress runs 4-5x faster per year and takeover risk is extremely high; his proposal for slowing down is a US-China compute transparency treaty.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI risk</span><span class=\"tag\">Superintelligence timelines</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"After GLP-1: Injection Is No Longer the Barrier, and Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era Injectables are no longer a dealbreaker, and peptide drugs are extending from weight loss into exercise mimetics and neural modulation. The scientist&#x27;s caveat: outside GLP, the data is still thin, and the approval cycle can run 20 years. From Our Neurons to Yours From Our Neurons to Yours After Ozempic: the revolution in peptide science | Jonathan Long Peptide drugs GLP-1 Exercise mimetics Weight loss Drug development Biotech GLP-1 (glucagon-like peptide-1) Lac-Phe (lactoyl-phenylalanine) Unimolecular polyagonist AGRP neurons (agouti-related peptide neurons) POMC neurons (pro-opiomelanocortin neurons) MOTRPAC (Molecular Transducers of Physical Activity Consortium)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-fromourneurons-glp-1之后-肽科学正在开启医学新大门/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/372ab8e78a5e10ef-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"F\"><span class=\"dur\">49:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">From Our Neurons to Yours</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>After GLP-1: Injection Is No Longer the Barrier, and Peptide Drugs Enter a New Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Injectables are no longer a dealbreaker, and peptide drugs are extending from weight loss into exercise mimetics and neural modulation. The scientist&#x27;s caveat: outside GLP, the data is still thin, and the approval cycle can run 20 years.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Peptide drugs</span><span class=\"tag\">GLP-1</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"High long-end yields aren&#x27;t policy weakness — AI data centers are bidding away the capital At Jackson Hole, Kansas City Fed President Schmid said the 30-year Treasury climbing above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for capital; his personal judgment is that monetary conditions are still on the loose side, and inflation running up into the mid-3s has to be dealt with. Odd Lots Odd Lots Kansas City Fed President Jeffrey Schmid on the First Jackson Hole of the Warsh Era Monetary policy Inflation Long-end rates Federal Reserve AI infrastructure Payments innovation atomic settlement r-star reaction function forward guidance dual mandate dissent\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-oddlots-长端利率高企不是政策软弱-是实体需求在抢资金/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/dbb9160e6d589beb-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">28:56</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>High long-end yields aren&#x27;t policy weakness — AI data centers are bidding away the capital</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">At Jackson Hole, Kansas City Fed President Schmid said the 30-year Treasury climbing above 5% is the result of AI and data centers competing for capital; his personal judgment is that monetary conditions are still on the loose side, and inflation running up into the mid-3s has to be dealt with.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Monetary policy</span><span class=\"tag\">Inflation</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"No Model of Their Own, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning on Interface and Speed Cursor&#x27;s success did not come from training its own model but from treating the product itself as the moat: no plugin, no early enterprise sales, no in-house model training — it came from behind on iteration speed, a hiring philosophy, and founder density. The a16z Show a16z Inside Cursor: The Anatomy of a Generational Startup Cursor AI coding Startup strategy Enterprise sales Talent acquisition Product philosophy self-serve back channel talent via acquisition monorepo\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-a16z-不做模型不早卖企业-用界面和速度赢下巨头/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/7b2f032532c3a865-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">39:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The a16z Show</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>No Model of Their Own, No Early Enterprise Sales: Winning on Interface and Speed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Cursor&#x27;s success did not come from training its own model but from treating the product itself as the moat: no plugin, no early enterprise sales, no in-house model training — it came from behind on iteration speed, a hiring philosophy, and founder density.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cursor</span><span class=\"tag\">AI coding</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era The most valuable thing an enterprise owns in the AI era is its data — Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s; AI agents holding legitimate credentials are the new top threat; and data infrastructure has to be rebuilt. No Priors No Priors Rethinking Legacy Data Infrastructure with Eon Co-Founders Ofir Ehrlich and Gonen Stein Data Assets AI Security Enterprise Data AI Agents Cloud Migration NHI / Non-Human Identity PLG / Product-Led Growth Data Foundation\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-nopriors-数据是-ai-时代唯一护城河-合法权限代理最危险/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c958bc100db9c41b-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">34:50</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">No Priors</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Google Paid $10 Million for a Bankrupt Airline&#x27;s Data: Data Is the Moat in the AI Era</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The most valuable thing an enterprise owns in the AI era is its data — Google paid $10 million for a bankrupt airline&#x27;s; AI agents holding legitimate credentials are the new top threat; and data infrastructure has to be rebuilt.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>4</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Data Assets</span><span class=\"tag\">AI Security</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"Index funds overpay 4% on rebalancing days; passive investing is more than indexing David Booth takes apart the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes a stock&#x27;s price up 4%, while real passive management means scientific portfolio construction plus careful execution. And the roughly 10% a century of equity returns rests on the human desire to make life better. The Rational Reminder Podcast Rational Reminder 50 Years of Evidence-Based Investing (with David Booth) | Rational Reminder 424 Passive investing Index funds Trading costs Investment philosophy Dimensional Jensen&#x27;s alpha soft dollar beta passive vs indexed\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-rationalreminder-指数基金为收盘价多付4-被动投资不只是指数/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/8948b5b54aada07c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">The Rational Reminder Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Index funds overpay 4% on rebalancing days; passive investing is more than indexing</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">David Booth takes apart the difference between index funds and passive investing: index rebalancing pushes a stock&#x27;s price up 4%, while real passive management means scientific portfolio construction plus careful execution. And the roughly 10% a century of equity returns rests on the human desire to make life better.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Passive investing</span><span class=\"tag\">Index funds</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"What the Fed Fears Most Is Not Tariffs but Unanchored Inflation Expectations Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest current risk: five and a half years above target, and an AI story that in the short run may push rates up rather than down. His key advice to rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market. Masters of Scale Masters of Scale Chicago Fed President on inflation, recession, and Trump’s attacks Inflation expectations Federal Reserve Interest rates AI bubble Productivity Macro policy Forward guidance Core inflation Unanchoring of inflation expectations Stagflation Lump of labor fallacy FOMC\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-masterscale-通胀预期失锚-美联储最怕的不是关税/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/b728f390557a15d4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"M\"><span class=\"dur\">35:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Masters of Scale</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>What the Fed Fears Most Is Not Tariffs but Unanchored Inflation Expectations</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Chicago Fed President Goolsbee points to inflation as the biggest current risk: five and a half years above target, and an AI story that in the short run may push rates up rather than down. His key advice to rate-sensitive industries is to watch the data, not the stock market.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Inflation expectations</span><span class=\"tag\">Federal Reserve</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"The sunscreen-is-toxic case falls apart: entering the bloodstream is not the same as harm — but the vitamin D risk is real The evidence most often cited for ‘sunscreen is toxic, sunscreen causes cancer’ mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causation; chemical filters showing up in blood does not make them poisonous. But daily use does raise the risk of winter vitamin D deficiency, and the evidence that sunscreen prevents melanoma in people with darker skin is especially thin. Science Vs Science Vs Sunscreen: Could It Be Toxic?? Sunscreen Vitamin D Melanoma Chemical filters Evidence-based medicine Skin cancer oxybenzone UV index Fitzpatrick scale SPF mineral sunscreen RCT\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-sciencevs-防晒霜不是毒药-但深肤色人群可能真不用防黑素瘤/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/a3282246c3202bd6-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">50:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>The sunscreen-is-toxic case falls apart: entering the bloodstream is not the same as harm — but the vitamin D risk is real</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The evidence most often cited for ‘sunscreen is toxic, sunscreen causes cancer’ mostly involves absurd doses or reversed causation; chemical filters showing up in blood does not make them poisonous. But daily use does raise the risk of winter vitamin D deficiency, and the evidence that sunscreen prevents melanoma in people with darker skin is especially thin.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Sunscreen</span><span class=\"tag\">Vitamin D</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\" data-hay=\"Without Plate Tectonics There Is No Life: Earth Is the Lucky Planet Plate tectonics remakes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed a fingernail grows, creating and destroying roughly 3 square kilometres of ocean crust every year. It shifts climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists at all — without it, there is none. In Our Time In Our Time Archive: Plate Tectonics Plate Tectonics Earth Science Origin of Life Scientific Revolutions Gaia Theory Planetary Habitability Benioff zone seafloor spreading Vine–Matthews hypothesis mantle plume black smoker Gaia theory\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-inourtime-没有板块构造就没有生命-地球是幸运的行星/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/c3f143ddc011b622-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"I\"><span class=\"dur\">44:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">In Our Time</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Without Plate Tectonics There Is No Life: Earth Is the Lucky Planet</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Plate tectonics remakes the Earth&#x27;s surface at the speed a fingernail grows, creating and destroying roughly 3 square kilometres of ocean crust every year. It shifts climate, isolates species, and even determines whether life exists at all — without it, there is none.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Plate Tectonics</span><span class=\"tag\">Earth Science</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"Ketogenic Diets for Psychiatric Illness: The Point Isn&#x27;t Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to relieve symptoms in psychiatric patients whom medication has failed; the mechanism is not just weight loss but a change in the brain&#x27;s energy metabolism and neurotransmitters, driven by mitophagy and mitochondrial biogenesis. Huberman Lab Huberman Lab Essentials: Diet &amp; Nutrition for Mental Health | Dr. Chris Palmer Ketogenic diet Mental health Mitochondria Low carb Clinical cases Mitophagy Mitochondrial biogenesis Ketosis Schizoaffective disorder\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-huberman-生酮饮食治疗难治性精神疾病-线粒体是核心/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/10ab5bacdd6a5f8c-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"H\"><span class=\"dur\">38:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Huberman Lab</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>Ketogenic Diets for Psychiatric Illness: The Point Isn&#x27;t Weight Loss, It&#x27;s Mitochondria</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Psychiatrist Chris Palmer uses ketogenic and low-carb diets to relieve symptoms in psychiatric patients whom medication has failed; the mechanism is not just weight loss but a change in the brain&#x27;s energy metabolism and neurotransmitters, driven by mitophagy and mitochondrial biogenesis.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>3</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Ketogenic diet</span><span class=\"tag\">Mental health</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"hist\" data-hay=\"15,000 People in a Cobalt Pit: Your Phone Battery Has Blood on It The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and electric vehicles depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to the assassination of Lumumba to a few dollars a day today, the same history of plunder is still running. Throughline Throughline The pillage that fueled America&#x27;s tech booms Cobalt Congo Supply chain Colonial history Electric vehicles Congo Free State Katanga Congo Reform Association\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-throughline-没有刚果的钴-就没有你的智能手机/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/19b38564c3e319e4-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">52:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">Throughline</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>15,000 People in a Cobalt Pit: Your Phone Battery Has Blood on It</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The lithium-ion batteries in smartphones and electric vehicles depend on Congolese cobalt; from Leopold II&#x27;s rubber terror to the assassination of Lumumba to a few dollars a day today, the same history of plunder is still running.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Cobalt</span><span class=\"tag\">Congo</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\" data-hay=\"AI detection is authorship identification, and the template-letter game is over AI detection is really authorship identification at scale, not sniffing out an ‘AI voice’. What actually gets broken are the social processes built on the assumption that writing a letter costs human labor — credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant review. Once the friction disappears, the institutions overload. Complex Systems Complex Systems How AI detection actually works, with Pangram’s Max Spero AI detection text provenance anti-spam public services programming careers model ethics anti-memetic property mode collapse cognitive surplus dead internet theory Jevons Paradox humanizer\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-complexsys-ai-检测的本质是作者识别-模板信博弈已失效/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d5836ffb777d7c36-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:04:54</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>AI detection is authorship identification, and the template-letter game is over</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AI detection is really authorship identification at scale, not sniffing out an ‘AI voice’. What actually gets broken are the social processes built on the assumption that writing a letter costs human labor — credit disputes, unemployment claims, grant review. Once the friction disappears, the institutions overload.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI detection</span><span class=\"tag\">text provenance</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\" data-hay=\"An open model passes through five or six intermediaries; the EU AI Act sees only one Open weights are not the same as open trustworthiness. Once an open source model has passed through five or six intermediaries, accountability blurs; the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap, and Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital. The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast Building Trustworthy Open Source AI - Sebnem Erener (EP 38) Open source EU AI Act AI governance Open source ethics European tech sovereignty Accountability chain zero trust open washing general purpose AI models layered responsibility\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-theinbetweente-开放权重不等于可信-五六个中介后谁来负责/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/99f7b27239fc36aa-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">23:46</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">The In-Between Tech and Trust Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>An open model passes through five or six intermediaries; the EU AI Act sees only one</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Open weights are not the same as open trustworthiness. Once an open source model has passed through five or six intermediaries, accountability blurs; the EU AI Act&#x27;s exemption design may leave a structural accountability gap, and Europe needs layered responsibility and patient capital.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Open source</span><span class=\"tag\">EU AI Act</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\" data-hay=\"India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics Was Not a Prelude to the Bomb: Postwar State Involvement Was the Watershed India&#x27;s early nuclear physics moved in step with Europe and America; the real watershed was the state stepping in after the war. Reading it backwards from the 1974 test only obscures how the TIFR model damaged universities and the wider scientific system. The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science Jahnavi Phalkey on India&#x27;s Place in the History of Modern Science History of Science India Big Science Nuclear Physics Cyclotron State and Science Tata Institute of Fundamental Research (TIFR) Department of Atomic Energy (DAE) Coulomb excitation Big Science global majority CERN\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-27-thehpspodcastc-别用1974年核试验倒读印度核物理史/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"/podcast/assets/cover/d0bc1b9f1ca1b0b2-c.jpg\" alt=\"\" decoding=\"async\" loading=\"lazy\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">46:27</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">The HPS Podcast - Conversations from History, Philosophy and Social Studies of Science</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0827</span></div>\n<h2>India&#x27;s Nuclear Physics Was Not a Prelude to the Bomb: Postwar State Involvement Was the Watershed</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">India&#x27;s early nuclear physics moved in step with Europe and America; the real watershed was the state stepping in after the war. Reading it backwards from the 1974 test only obscures how the TIFR model damaged universities and the wider scientific system.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">History of Science</span><span class=\"tag\">India</span>\n</div></div></a>"]
